    "num": "§ 01",
    "original": "But, you may say, we asked you to speak about women and fiction—what has that got to do with a room of one's own? I will try to explain. When you asked me to speak about women and fiction I sat down on the banks of a river and began to wonder what the words meant. They might mean simply a few remarks about Fanny Burney; a few more about Jane Austen; a tribute to the Brontës and a sketch of Haworth Parsonage under snow; some witticisms if possible about Miss Mitford; a respectful allusion to George Eliot; a reference to Mrs. Gaskell and one would have done. But at second sight the words seemed not so simple. The title women and fiction might mean, and you may have meant it to mean, women and what they are like; or it might mean women and the fiction that they write; or it might mean women and the fiction that is written about them; or it might mean that somehow all three are inextricably mixed together and you want me to consider them in that light. But when I began to consider the subject in this last way, which seemed the most interesting, I soon saw that it had one fatal drawback. I should never be able to come to a conclusion. I should never be able to fulfil what is, I understand, the first duty of a lecturer—to hand you after an hour's discourse a nugget of pure truth to wrap up between the pages of your notebooks and keep on the mantelpiece for ever. All I could do was to offer you an opinion upon one minor point—a woman must have money and a room of her own if she is to write fiction; and that, as you will see, leaves the great problem of the true nature of woman and the true nature of fiction unsolved. I have shirked the duty of coming to a conclusion upon these two questions—women and fiction remain, so far as I am concerned, unsolved problems. But in order to make some amends I am going to do what I can to show you how I arrived at this opinion about the room and the money. I am going to develop in your presence as fully and freely as I can the train of thought which led me to think this. Perhaps if I lay bare the ideas, the prejudices, that lie behind this statement you will find that they have some bearing upon women and some upon fiction. At any rate, when a subject is highly controversial—and any question about sex is that—one cannot hope to tell the truth. One can only show how one came to hold whatever opinion one does hold. One can only give one's audience the chance of drawing their own conclusions as they observe the limitations, the prejudices, the idiosyncrasies of the speaker. Fiction here is likely to contain more truth than fact. Therefore I propose, making use of all the liberties and licences of a novelist, to tell you the story of the two days that preceded my coming here—how, bowed down by the weight of the subject which you have laid upon my shoulders, I pondered it, and made it work in and out of my daily life. I need not say that what I am about to describe has no existence; Oxbridge is an invention; so is Fernham; \"I\" is only a convenient term for somebody who has no real being. Lies will flow from my lips, but there may perhaps be some truth mixed up with them; it is for you to seek out this truth and to decide whether any part of it is worth keeping. If not, you will of course throw the whole of it into the waste-paper basket and forget all about it.",
    "zh": "不过，你们或许会说，我们[请你来谈的是女性与小说]{#g-rw-c1-01}——这和一间只属于自己的房间有什么关系呢？[^rw-fn-c1-1]我试着解释一下吧。当你们请我来谈女性与小说时，我走到一条河的岸边坐下来，开始琢磨这几个字究竟意味着什么。它们或许只是意味着就[范妮·伯尼]{#g-rw-c1-02}略发几句议论；就简·奥斯汀再多谈几句；向勃朗特姐妹致一番敬意，顺带勾勒几笔[白雪覆盖下的霍沃斯牧师住宅]{#g-rw-c1-03}；倘若做得到，再讲几句关于[米特福德小姐]{#g-rw-c1-04}的俏皮话；恭敬地提及乔治·埃利奥特；顺便引述一下盖斯凯尔夫人，这差事就算交待过去了。可再细想一层，这几个字似乎又没有那样简单。「女性与小说」这个题目，或许意味着——你们原本的用意或许也正是——女性以及她们究竟是什么样的人；又或许意味着女性以及她们所写的小说；又或许意味着女性以及别人写她们的那些小说；甚或意味着这三者不知怎的难解难分地交织在一起，而你们希望我从这个角度来加以考察。可是，当我着手以最后这种方式——这似乎也是最饶有趣味的方式——来思索这个题目时，我很快看出了它有一个致命的缺陷：我将永远无法得出一个结论。我将永远无法履行据我所知身为演讲者的第一天职——在一小时的讲演之后，递给你们一块纯金般的真理，好让你们夹进笔记本的书页里包好，带回去永远供在壁炉架上。我所能做的，只是就一个枝节问题向你们提出一点看法——**女人要想写小说，就必须有钱，还要有一间只属于自己的房间**；而正如你们将会看到的，这就让女性的真正本质与小说的真正本质这两个重大问题依旧悬而未决。我逃避了就这两个问题得出结论的职责——就我而言，女性与小说依然是未解的难题。但为了略作弥补，我打算尽我所能向你们展示，我是怎样得出关于房间与金钱的这番看法的。我要当着你们的面，尽可能充分而自由地铺陈那条引我生出这一想法的思绪脉络。或许，倘若我把隐藏在这一论断背后的种种念头与偏见坦露出来，你们会发现它们多少与女性有些干系，也多少与小说有些干系。无论如何，只要一个题目极具争议——而任何涉及性别的问题莫不如此——人便不能指望说出纯粹的真理。人只能展示自己是如何一步步抱定眼下所持的看法的。人只能让听众在审视演讲者的局限、偏见与癖性之际，获得自行得出结论的机会。在这里，虚构往往比事实包含更多的真实。因此我打算充分动用小说家的一切自由与特权，向你们讲述我来到此地之前那两天的故事——讲讲我如何被你们压在肩头的这个题目坠得弯下了腰，一路思索着它，让它在我的日常生活里往复穿行。我不必声明，我即将描绘的一切在现实中并不存在；[奥克斯布里奇纯属虚构；费恩汉姆也是如此]{#g-rw-c1-05}；「我」不过是一个权宜的代称，指代某个并无真实存在的人。谎言将从我唇间流淌而出，但里头或许也掺杂着几分真实；这真实要靠你们自己去寻觅，去裁断其中是否有哪一部分值得留存。倘若没有，你们当然会把这一切统统扔进废纸篓，忘得一干二净。",
